  "content": {
    "title": "Portland Trail Blazers vs Phoenix Suns predictions, odds: Who wins NBA play-in game?",
    "text": "April 14, 2026, 4:02 p.m. CTThe Phoenix Suns will host the Portland Trail Blazers in the NBA Play-in Tournament Tuesday at Mortgage Matchup Center.The winner will advance as the Western Conference's No. 7 seed in the NBA Playoffs, where it will face the second-seeded San Antonio Spurs in the first round.Here's a look at how the Suns-Trail Blazers game is projected to play out tonight:Order book on the Thunder's road to NBA titleWhat time is Portland Trail Blazers vs Phoenix Suns game in NBA Play-In Tournament? When is Suns-Trail Blazers game tonight?Date: Tuesday, April 14Time: 9 p.m. CTWhere: Mortgage Matchup Center in PhoenixThe Portland vs Phoenix game starts at 9 p.m. CT Tuesday from Mortgage Matchup Center in Phoenix.What channel is Portland Trail Blazers vs Phoenix Suns game tonight? How to watch, stream Suns-Trail BlazersLivestream: Amazon Prime (here's how to stream it live)The Suns vs Trail Blazers NBA Play-In Tournament game can be streamed on Prime.2026 NBA play-in tournament scheduleAll times are Central. All play-in games are on Amazon Prime (see how to stream it live):Tuesday, April 14East: No. 9 Charlotte Hornets vs. No. 10 Miami Heat, 6:30 p.m. (Elimination game)West: No. 7 Phoenix Suns vs. No. 8 Portland Trail Blazers, 9 p.m. (Winner earns No. 7 seed)Wednesday, April 15East: No. 7 Philadelphia 76ers vs. No. 8 Orlando Magic, 6:30 p.m. (Winner earns No. 7 seed)West: No. 9 Los Angeles Clippers vs. No. 10 Golden State Warriors, 9 p.m. (Elimination game)Friday, April 17East: 7/8 loser vs. 9/10 winner, 6:30 p.m. (Winner earns No. 8 seed)West: 7/8 loser vs. 9/10 winner, 9 p.m. (Winner earns No. 8 seed)2026 NBA play-in tournament predictionsEastern ConferenceGame 1: No. 9 Charlotte Hornets vs. No. 10 Miami Heat (Elimination game)Jenni Carlson, columnist: Miami 110, Charlotte 105Joe Mussatto, columnist: Charlotte 111, Miami 96 Justin Martinez, Thunder writer: Charlotte 116, Miami 111Game 2: No. 7. Philadelphia 76ers vs. No. 8 Orlando Magic (Winner earns No. 7 seed)Jenni Carlson, columnist: Philadelphia 119, Orlando 109Joe Mussatto, columnist: Philadelphia 108, Orlando 104 Justin Martinez, Thunder writer: Philadelphia 110, Orlando 108Game 3: Winner of Game 1 vs. Loser of Game 2 (Winner earns No. 8 seed)Jenni Carlson, columnist: Orlando 121, Miami 119Joe Mussatto, columnist: Charlotte 102, Orlando 90 Justin Martinez, Thunder writer: Charlotte 120, Orlando 112Western ConferenceGame 1: No. 9 Los Angeles Clippers vs. No. 10 Golden State Warriors (Elimination game)Jenni Carlson, columnist: Los Angeles Clippers 107, Golden State 102Joe Mussatto, columnist: Los Angeles Clippers 117, Golden State 98 Justin Martinez, Thunder writer: Los Angeles Clippers 118, Golden State 108Game 2: No. 7 Phoenix Suns vs. No. 8 Portland Trail Blazers (Winner earns No. 7 seed)Jenni Carlson, columnist: Phoenix 101, Portland 97Joe Mussatto, columnist: Portland 102, Phoenix 99 Justin Martinez, Thunder writer: Phoenix 113, Portland 109Game 3: Winner of Game 1 vs. Loser of Game 2 (Winner earns No. 8 seed)Jenni Carlson, columnist: Los Angeles Clippers 104, Portland 102Joe Mussatto, columnist: Los Angeles Clippers 106, Phoenix 103 Justin Martinez, Thunder writer: Los Angeles Clippers 107, Portland 105Which play-in tournament team is biggest threat in NBA playoffs?Jenni Carlson, columnist: The Clippers have some really good individual talent, starting with Kawhi Leonard. He’s good enough to win one, maybe even two games by himself. But the Clippers play slow. Will they be able to keep up with the spry, speedy Thunder? Younger guys like Darius Garland, John Collins and Bennedict Mathurin are going to have to get back on defense and get out in transition when possible.Joe Mussatto, columnist: It's the Hornets. Since the All-Star break, Charlotte has the second-best net rating in the NBA behind only San Antonio. The Hornets have been playing better basketball for the last 30ish games than the Pistons — the No. 1 seed Charlotte would face. The Hornets are going to be a trendy pick to upset the Pistons if that's the matchup. Justin Martinez, Thunder beat writer: I also think the Hornets are the biggest threat, but I'll give a shoutout to the Suns. San Antonio is the better team on paper by a sizable margin, but it's an inexperienced group that would have its mental and physical toughness tested by Phoenix. The Suns have gritty defenders at every position, an elite bucket getter in Devin Booker and some other scoring options in Dillon Brooks and Jalen Green. I could see that being a six-game series before San Antonio closes it out.Justin Martinez covers the Thunder and NBA for The Oklahoman. Have a story idea for Justin? He can be reached at jmartinez@oklahoman.com or on X/Twitter at @Justintohoops. Sign up for theThunder Sports Minute newsletter to access more NBA coverage. Support Justin's work and that of other Oklahoman journalists by purchasing adigital subscription today at subscribe.oklahoman.com.Our editors independently choose our recommendations. Some content is produced with paid support from a third party, however our editorial decisions remain independent. If you buy through our links, the USA TODAY Network may earn a commission. Prices and availability may change.",
